You ask an AI tool to "sum all numbers in an array" and it returns:

```js
function sumArray(arr) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= arr.length; i++) {
    total += arr[i];
  }
  return total;
}
```

It compiles. But `sumArray([2, 3])` returns `NaN`. You spend five minutes stepping through it before noticing: the loop starts at `i = 1` and uses `<=`, so it reads past the end of the array on the last iteration. A classic off-by-one — and one you could have caught in ten seconds if you knew what to scan for.

